    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d294">

                <pb facs="0294" n="282" xml:id="pg282"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/7761: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Bulgaria</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Earle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sofia</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-01-21T16:00:00+02:00">January 21, 1941—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 21—3:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>27. After an interview with the Minister for Foreign Affairs and the Minister of
                    War, Colonel Donovan<anchor n="28" xml:id="d294-f1"/> feels that the Bulgarian
                    Government expects Germany to occupy Bulgaria; that they are very unhappy about
                    it but that they will not resist this occupation. I concur in this opinion.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Earle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d294-f1" n="28" xml:id="d294-f1-content">
                <p>William J. Donovan, Unofficial Observer of the Secretary of the Navy of the
                    United States.</p>
            </note>
